        "math_academy_id": "q-129180",
        "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{3}{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e8-2,\\,4,\\,\\frac{2}{3}\u27e9.$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\u27e8-2,\\,4,\\,\\frac{2}{3}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82,\\,-4,\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the scalar multiplication rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{3}{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{3}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \u27e82,\\,-4,\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e83,\\,-6,\\,-1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
        "deliberately_incorrect_submission": false,
